 deep(['key-locality', 'uuid-versions', 'key-size', 'key-exposure'],
  'What a primary key choice changes underneath',
  ['Every table needs a primary key that never changes. A generated surrogate key is the usual choice, because natural values such as email change. The open question is what kind of generated value to use, and the main options are a database sequence (bigint), a random UUID (version 4), and a time-ordered UUID (version 7) or similar time-first id.',
   'The primary-key index is a B-tree sorted by key. With a sequence, each new key is the largest so far, so inserts always go to the rightmost leaf. That page and its parents stay in memory, and full pages split at the edge, leaving older pages almost full.',
   'With a random key, the insert position is uniform across the index. While the index fits in memory this is cheap. Once it does not, the chance that the target leaf is cached is roughly memory ÷ index size, and every miss is a random read before the insert can finish. Splits happen in the middle of the tree, which leaves pages part empty, so the index is larger and fits memory even less.',
   'A time-ordered key restores locality: its leading bits are a timestamp, so keys created in the same moment sit together at the right edge. It keeps the property that matters for distributed systems: any machine or offline client can create one without asking the database, and the random tail avoids collisions.',
   'Width matters because the key is copied everywhere it is referenced: foreign key columns in child tables, their indexes and, in InnoDB, every secondary index of the table itself. Going from 8 to 16 bytes adds 8 bytes to each of those copies, and storing UUIDs as text roughly doubles that again.',
   'Exposure is a separate decision. Sequence values leak volume and make enumeration trivial; time-ordered ids leak creation time; random ids leak nothing. Many systems use an internal bigint for joins and a separate public id, which keeps joins compact and URLs opaque.'],
  ['Use a bigint sequence when one database creates all ids and they are not shown to outsiders',
   'Use UUIDv7 when ids must be created by many services or offline clients and the table is insert-heavy',
   'Use an internal bigint plus a separate public id when you want compact joins and opaque URLs',
   'Use a time-ordered id when recent rows are read far more than old ones'],
  ['Avoid random UUIDv4 as the primary key of a large, insert-heavy table that will outgrow memory',
   'Avoid UUIDv7 where revealing an id\'s creation time is a problem',
   'Avoid storing UUIDs as text when the engine has a native UUID or 16-byte binary type',
   'Avoid using the time order of ids as the business order of events across machines'],
  ['Key', 'Size', 'Insert locality', 'Created without the database', 'Guessable'],
  [['bigint sequence', '8 bytes', 'Right edge', 'No', 'Yes'],
   ['UUIDv4', '16 bytes', 'Random', 'Yes', 'No'],
   ['UUIDv7', '16 bytes', 'Right edge (by time)', 'Yes', 'Reveals time, not the next value'],
   ['UUID as text', '36+ bytes', 'Depends on version', 'Yes', 'Depends on version']],
  'Cache misses from random keys',
  ['Primary-key index 40 GB', '8 GB of memory available for its pages', '2,000 inserts a second', 'Illustrative figures'],
  ['Chance a random leaf is cached ≈ 8 ÷ 40 = 0.2.',
   'Misses per second ≈ (1 − 0.2) × 2,000 = 1,600 random reads.',
   'At about 0.1 ms per SSD random read, that is 1,600 × 0.1 ms = 160 ms of storage time every second, before any query reads.',
   'With sequential or time-ordered keys, the rightmost leaf is hot, so misses are close to zero.'],
  'The same insert rate costs about 1,600 extra storage reads a second with random keys and almost none with ordered ones, and the gap grows as the index grows.',
  'RFC 9562 and the InnoDB index documentation', 'Official documentation',
  'RFC 9562, published by the IETF in 2024, replaced RFC 4122 and defined UUID versions 6, 7 and 8. It describes version 7 as a 48-bit Unix millisecond timestamp followed by random bits, and recommends it over older versions for new database keys because it sorts by time. '
  'The MySQL manual explains that InnoDB stores rows in the clustered primary-key index and that every secondary index record contains the primary key columns, which is why it advises keeping the primary key short.',
  [('Switching to UUIDv4 keys because ids must be created offline', 'Use UUIDv7: offline creation without scattering inserts'),
   ('Hiding sequence ids instead of checking ownership', 'Opaque ids reduce guessing; the server must still check that the caller owns the row'),
   ('Storing UUIDs as 36-character text', 'Use the native type: 16 bytes and fast comparisons')],
  ['Random keys scatter inserts; once the index outgrows memory most inserts read from storage',
   'UUIDv7 is created anywhere and still sorts by time',
   'The key is copied into every foreign key and, in InnoDB, every secondary index, so width matters',
   'Exposure is its own decision: internal bigint plus public id is a common split']),
 predict('How often is the leaf already cached?',
  'A primary-key index on random UUIDv4 keys is 60 GB. 15 GB of memory holds its pages, and inserts arrive at a steady rate. Roughly what share of inserts find their target leaf page already in memory?',
  [('a', 'About 75%', 'That is the miss rate: three quarters of the index is not in memory.'),
   ('b', 'Nearly all, as with a sequence', 'A sequence keeps hitting the same hot page; random keys pick any leaf.'),
   ('c', 'About 25%', 'Right: a uniformly random leaf is cached with a probability of about 15 ÷ 60.')],
  'c',
  'With random keys, the cache hit chance for inserts is about memory ÷ index size, so it keeps falling as the table grows. Ordered keys keep it near 100% at any size.'),
